## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a simple, reliable streaming ingest with validation, a practical approach to schema evolution, and basic operations like dead-lettering, archiving, rollback, and monitoring at beginner level.

## Key Concepts

- Pub/Sub as a source and dead-lettering for bad messages
- Dataflow (Beam) for parsing/validation
- BigQuery core schema + extras JSON field for evolution
- Idempotent writes using insertId or MERGE
- Rollback strategies using Pub/Sub replay or GCS archive
- Basic monitoring and alerts for data quality

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-beam style pseudocode
// Read from Pub/Sub, parse JSON, validate required fields, emit rows to BigQuery
class ParseAndValidateDoFn extends DoFn {
  processElement(ctx) {
    const msg = JSON.parse(ctx.element);
    if (!msg.timestamp || !msg.user_id || !msg.endpoint) return;
    const row = {core_field1: msg.timestamp, core_field2: msg.user_id, core_field3: msg.endpoint, extras: JSON.stringify(msg.extras || {})};
    yield row;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test schema-evolution choices and measure their impact on query performance?
- What would you monitor to detect data quality regressions in this pipeline?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of data privacy, per-tenant isolation, and practical use of GCP data tools in a beginner-friendly setup.

## Key Concepts

- Data masking in streaming + Data Catalog lineage
- Per-tenant isolation with BigQuery datasets
- Rollback using backup tables and views
- Schema drift handling and late data tolerance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-Dataflow DoFn masking PII
class MaskPiIFn extends DoFn {
  processElement(ctx) {
    const record = ctx.element;
    record.email = mask(record.email);
    record.phone = mask(record.phone);
    return record;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test data contracts in Data Catalog?
- How would you scale this pattern to 10s of tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a scalable, privacy-conscious cross-tenant pipeline on GCP, integrating Pub/Sub, Dataflow, BigQuery, Data Catalog, Data Exchange, and CMEK/DLP. It also probes rollback, cross-region resilience, and cost governance.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-tenant isolation and CMEK key management
- Real-time ingestion with Pub/Sub and Dataflow
- Per-tenant masking via Cloud DLP and access controls
- End-to-end lineage and data contracts in Data Catalog
- Sharing aggregates via BigQuery Data Exchange
- 24h per-tenant rollback via Pub/Sub retention topic
- Cross-region failover and schema evolution
- Cost governance and budget controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: per-tenant masking placeholder (not production-ready)
function maskTenantRow(row, tenantConfig) {
  // Apply tenant-specific masking rules, preserving schema
  const masked = { ...row };
  if (tenantConfig.maskEmail) masked.email = redact(masked.email);
  if (tenantConfig.maskPii) masked.ssn = null;
  return masked;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify rollback consistency for all tenants within 24h?
- What are the key failure modes during cross-region failover and how would you mitigate them?
